  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>An IPC audit checks the care environment against the code of practice on the prevention and control of infections, made under the Health and Social Care Act 2008</li><li>The three fronts are hand hygiene, PPE, and the cleaning and cleanability of the environment</li><li>The audit is about the environment and process, observed on the floor; it is not a resident's clinical record</li><li>The audit does not stop an outbreak; acting on the findings does, and the registered manager owns the actions</li><li>Quickler captures the walk and evidence and gives the group a live dashboard across every home</li></ul></div><h2>What an IPC audit in a care home is for</h2><p>Infection spreads through a care home along ordinary routes: hands, surfaces, shared equipment, waste. An IPC audit walks those routes before an outbreak does, checking that the controls a home relies on are actually there and working. It checks the home against the code of practice on the prevention and control of infections, made under the Health and Social Care Act 2008, and CQC assesses IPC as part of the safe key question.</p><p>The audit does not stop an outbreak. Acting on the findings does. What the audit does is catch the empty dispenser, the bare PPE cupboard and the cleaning schedule that is three days behind, evidence each with a photo, and let the group see which home carries which risk.</p><h2>The three fronts of an IPC audit</h2><h3>1. Hand hygiene</h3><p>The single most important control. The audit checks the enablers, hand-wash basins with soap and paper towels, alcohol gel at the point of care, no blocked or broken dispensers, and observes actual practice: are staff cleaning their hands at the right moments? Findings are recorded at the point of observation with photos.</p><h3>2. PPE</h3><p>Gloves, aprons, masks and eye protection: available and stocked, stored correctly, in date, and put on, removed and disposed of properly. The audit records stock and observed use, with a photo of the station.</p><h3>3. Environment and cleaning</h3><p>Cleaning schedules actually followed, surfaces and floors intact and cleanable rather than cracked or worn, equipment decontaminated between residents, and waste and laundry segregated and handled safely. Anything that cannot be cleaned properly is photographed and flagged for replacement.</p><h2>The standard, and how it varies by nation</h2><p>In England, IPC in registered care sits under the code of practice on the prevention and control of infections, linked to the CQC fundamental standards. The rest of the UK uses different regulators, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland, Care Inspectorate Wales in Wales and RQIA in Northern Ireland, and their own IPC guidance. Build the audit around the framework that applies to your home, work from the current published code and guidance, and treat this as general information, not clinical or legal advice.</p><h2>What to look for in an IPC audit tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-the-floor completion:</strong> the walk recorded as it happens, not written up later</li><li><strong>Photo evidence per finding:</strong> the empty dispenser or cracked worktop captured with a location and a timestamp</li><li><strong>A dashboard across homes:</strong> a regional IPC lead sees every home's position without chasing spreadsheets</li><li><strong>Action list out of the box:</strong> each gap becomes an action with an owner and a date</li><li><strong>Right scope:</strong> the tool audits the environment and process; it is not a clinical record and no resident-identifiable data is entered</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the IPC environment walk and evidence and turns it into an action list, with a dashboard across homes. It does not stop an outbreak, it does not manage infection cases, care plans or medication, and it is not a clinical record. The registered manager acting on the findings carries the outcome. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is an IPC audit in a care home?</h3><p>It is an infection prevention and control audit of the care environment: hand hygiene enablers and practice, PPE availability and use, and cleaning and cleanability of the premises and equipment. It checks the home against the code of practice on the prevention and control of infections, made under the Health and Social Care Act 2008, and CQC assesses IPC as part of the safe key question.</p><h3>How often should a care home do an IPC audit?</h3><p>There is no single statutory frequency; many homes run a monthly or quarterly IPC environment audit alongside daily cleaning checks, with more frequent audits during an outbreak. Follow your own IPC policy and the current code of practice and regulator guidance, which are updated over time.</p><h3>Does Quickler manage infection cases or medication?</h3><p>No. Quickler audits the environment and process: dispensers, PPE stock, cleaning schedules and observed practice, with photos. It is not a clinical record and does not manage individual infection cases, isolation, care plans or medication. Those stay in your care management system. Quickler is the environment and evidence audit around it, with a live dashboard across homes.</p><h3>Can I run an IPC audit over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The IPC lead receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the report and action list generate automatically. No separate app or login is required, and no resident-identifiable data is entered, because quickler is not a clinical record.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-audit-software-uk.html\">Care home audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/cqc-compliance-audit-checklist-uk.html\">CQC compliance audit checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-health-safety-audit-uk.html\">Care home health and safety audit</a></li></ul>",
